# Natural-language retrieval judgments — the queries golden.jsonl does not contain.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. golden.jsonl grades catalog items by NAME, and its queries
# carry the vocabulary of the docs they target ("generate algorithmic art with
# p5.js flow fields"). That is BM25's home ground: measured over the pinned
# corpus it scores recall 1.000 there, which CLAUDE.md records as expected. So
# the set cannot distinguish retrieval engines on the thing the keyless dense
# work was built for — a user describing a PROBLEM in their own words.
#
# METHOD, stated so it can be checked rather than trusted:
#   1. Every query was written from the target's own `description` field and
#      nothing else. No retrieval was run against any engine while composing —
#      the whole set was written first and scored once, so the queries could not
#      be selected to flatter a result already seen. That ordering is the point;
#      see keyless-semantic-search-for-everyone for why fitting and reporting on
#      one set is the failure mode here.
#   2. Queries deliberately AVOID the target's distinctive name tokens. A query
#      containing "docker" finds docker-expert by string match and measures
#      nothing. "my containers keep restarting" is the question a user actually
#      has.
#   3. Where two items genuinely serve one intent (mcp-builder and build-mcp are
#      near-duplicates in this corpus), both are listed relevant. Grading one as
#      wrong would penalise an engine for a corpus duplication, not a retrieval
#      failure.
#
# HOW TO RUN. Needs the pinned corpus (scripts/ensure_eval_corpus.sh) and, for
# the dense/hybrid columns, the [rag] extra plus a built store:
#   python3 scripts/eval_retrieval.py --golden tests/eval/golden-natural.jsonl -k 10
# NOT wired into `make eval` or the required gate: that gate must stay
# deterministic and offline-ish, and this set is for comparing engines rather
# than flooring one. Adding it to the gate would also floor a number nobody has
# argued for yet.
#
# GRADING. A row may pin an `exemplar` — "tap::skill_md", the entry the query was
# written about — and is then graded on that entry's CONTENT CLASS: byte-identical
# mirrors from other registries still count, a different skill sharing the name
# does not. 28 of these 50 rows carry one. They were not chosen: over the pinned
# corpus each of their relevant names resolves to exactly ONE body, so the pin is
# a lookup rather than a judgment, and the pinned taps.txt is what makes that
# reproducible.
#
# The other 22 are genuinely undecided. `code-reviewer` is 13 different skills
# here, `update-docs` 10, `commit` 4, and their descriptions share a median
# similarity of ~0.15 — there is no "same skill re-published" shortcut. Deciding
# which one a question meant is a statement about intent, and guessing it would
# bake one opinion into a published number. To see the menu:
#
#   python3 scripts/eval_retrieval.py --golden tests/eval/golden-natural.jsonl --worksheet
#
# which prints every candidate body and its description. It is generated rather
# than listed here on purpose: the candidate set is a fact about the corpus that
# is tapped, so written down it would be wrong the first time a pin moves.
#
# One JSON object per line: {query, relevant[], exemplar?, kind, note}
